4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2

In the trp leader, alternative RNA structures compete: 1-2 (or ribosome coverage of 2) vs. 2-3 (antiterminator) vs. 3-4 (terminator). Under high tryptophan the ribosome does not stall, covers region 2, and thereby permits 3-4 formation. A mutation that abolishes 1-2 pairing frees region 2 to pair with region 3 even when the ribosome is not stalled, thereby sequestering region 3 and blocking formation of the 3-4 terminator hairpin. Consequently transcription continues. The other options either favor 3-4 formation (B), do not affect hairpin formation itself (C), or reduce overall transcription (E).}

}

3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator depends on mutually exclusive RNA secondary structures in the trpL leader transcript. Regions 1–2 can pair, regions 2–3 can pair (antiterminator), and regions 3–4 can pair (intrinsic terminator). Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome does not stall at the tandem Trp codons inside region 1; it therefore covers the proximal portion of region 2 and blocks formation of the 2–3 antiterminator, freeing region 3 to pair with region 4 and terminate transcription.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from base-pairing with region 2 removes the competing 1–2 structure. Region 2 is therefore available to pair with region 3 even when the ribosome has advanced through region 1. Consequently the 2–3 antiterminator forms constitutively, 3–4 pairing is precluded, and transcription continues through the operon regardless of tryptophan level.  

The other options either strengthen termination (B, C), reduce overall transcription (E), or have no direct effect on hairpin choice (D).}

